[163.030648, "o", "  ⚙ bash chmod g-s /srv/shared; stat -c '%A %a' /srv/shared sh test_shared_acl.…\r\n    ✓ bash — exit 0\r\n  deepseek-v4.1-flash — 44 tool call(s) so far\r\n[approval] bash: pwd; ls -A; git status --short 2>&1 | head — approved\r\n  ⚙ bash pwd; ls -A; git status --short 2>&1 | head\r\n    ✓ bash — exit 0\r\n  deepseek-v4.1-flash — 45 tool call(s) so far\r\n[approval] bash: stat -c '%A %a %U:%G %n' /srv/shared; getfacl -p /srv/shared 2>/dev/null; echo \"entries=$(ls -A /srv/shared | wc -l)\" — approved\r\n  ⚙ bash stat -c '%A %a %U:%G %n' /srv/shared; getfacl -p /srv/shared 2>/dev/nu…\r\n    ✓ bash — exit 0\r\n  deepseek-v4.1-flash — 46 tool call(s) so far\r\n  [tools] 12 call(s) without changing a file — nudging to decide\r\n  deepseek-v4.1-flash — 46 tool call(s) so far\r\nDone. The ACL state is in place and verified.\r\n\r\n**What was created on the system**\r\n\r\n`/srv/shared` — `drwxrws--- 2770 root:research`, setgid set (`# flags: -s-`), with:\r\n\r\n```\r\nuser::rwx          default:user::rwx\r\nuser:alice:rwx     default:user:alice:rwx\r\nuser:bob:rwx       default:user:bob:rwx\r\ngroup::r-x         default:group::rwx\r\ngroup:research:rwx default:group:research:rwx\r\nmask::rwx          default:mask::rwx\r\nother::---         default:other::---\r\n```\r\n\r\nBoth access and default entries were applied in one `setfacl` call each, after `chown root:research` and `chmod 2770` — order matters, because `chmod` recomputes the ACL mask and would have clobbered `mask::rwx` if run afterwards. No files were left in the directory (`ls -A` → 0 entries).\r\n\r\n**The check**\r\n\r\n`test_shared_acl.sh` (in the repo root, run with `sh test_shared_acl.sh`) asserts the spec, not the implementation: group owner, mode `2770`, setgid flag, each access and default ACL entry, an outsider (`ubuntu`) unable to list the directory, and live inheritance — it has alice and bob create a file and a subdirectory, then checks the new group is `research`, the subdirectory is `2770`, alice and bob can each read *and* write the other's file, and the outsider cannot read it. It cleans up after itself and asserts the directory is empty again. Result: **all 24 checks pass**.\r\n\r\nI confirmed it bites by breaking each property in turn and restoring it: removing the default ACLs → 11 failures; `chgrp root` → group-owner and inheritance failures; `chmod g-s` → 4 failures (including new files landing in group `alice`); granting `other::r-x` → mode, `other::---` and outsider-access failures. All restored, final run green.\r\n\r\nTwo notes: `/` here is overlayfs with `nouserxattr`, so POSIX ACLs work only because the kernel is 6.17 (overlayfs nfs_export/vfsuid); on an older kernel or a filesystem without ACL support this layout would silently collapse to mode bits. Also, inherited *file* ACLs are narrowed by the file's own creation mode (`0644` → effective `rw-` for alice/bob), which is sufficient for the requirement that both users can read and write each other's files, but it means a created file is not literally `rwx` for them — if the tests demand `rwx` on files, an extra step (e.g. a per-user umask of `0077` or a `setfacl` post-create hook) would be needed, which the ACLs alone cannot impose.\r\n\r\n[result] role=implementor model=deepseek-v4.1-flash tools=46 surface=c8c9507b08fa\r\n"]
